3. Abstraction and Interfaces: To implement abstraction using abstract classes and interfaces.

3.a) Create an abstract class Shape with abstract method area().

Aim:
To create an abstract class Shape with an abstract method area() and implement it in a subclass.

PROGRAM :- 

abstract class Shape {
    abstract void area();   // Abstract method
}
class Rectangle extends Shape {
    int length = 10;
    int width = 5;
    void area() {
        System.out.println("Area = " + (length * width));
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rectangle r = new Rectangle();
        r.area();
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Area = 50


3.b) Implement subclasses Circle and Rectangle. 

Aim
To implement Circle and Rectangle subclasses from an abstract class Shape and calculate their areas.

PROGRAM :- 

abstract class Shape {
    abstract void area();
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius = 5;

    void area() {
        System.out.println("Area of Circle = " + (3.14 * radius * radius));
    }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
    int length = 10;
    int width = 5;

    void area() {
        System.out.println("Area of Rectangle = " + (length * width));
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape c = new Circle();
        Shape r = new Rectangle();

        c.area();
        r.area();
    }
}

OUTPUT :- 

Area of Circle = 78.5
Area of Rectangle = 50


3.c) Create an interface Printable and implement it in a class. 

Aim:
To create an interface Printable and implement it in a class to demonstrate the use of interfaces in Java.

PROGRAM :- 

// Interface
interface Printable {
    void print();   // Abstract method
}

// Class implementing the interface
class Document implements Printable {

    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing the document...");
    }
}

// Main class
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Document d = new Document();
        d.print();
    }
}

Output :-

Printing the document...

3.d) Demonstrate multiple inheritance using interfaces.

Aim:
To demonstrate multiple inheritance in Java using interfaces.


Program :- 

// First Interface
interface Animal {
    void sound();
}

// Second Interface
interface Bird {
    void fly();
}

// Class implementing both interfaces
class Bat implements Animal, Bird {

    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bat makes sound.");
    }

    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Bat can fly.");
    }
}

// Main class
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bat b = new Bat();
        b.sound();
        b.fly();
    }
}

OUTPUT:- 

Bat makes sound.
Bat can fly.

